19:T6e8,## One reason to change

The **S** in SOLID:

> **A class should have only one reason to change.**

Read “reason to change” as **one actor / one concern** owning the class. If the billing team, the DBA, the design team, and the marketing team could each independently force you to edit the *same* class, it has four reasons to change, and that's three too many.

## The god-class smell

```
class Invoice {
    calcTotal(){ ... }   // business math   ← finance
    saveToDB(){ ... }    // persistence     ← DBA / schema
    toPdf(){ ... }       // layout          ← design
    emailTo(c){ ... }    // delivery        ← marketing
}
```

This one class mixes **business rules**, **persistence**, **formatting**, and **email**. Four unrelated concerns sharing one body of code is the **god-class** smell.

Why it hurts: every concern is now **coupled** through the file. Tweak the PDF margins and you risk the tax math; migrate the database schema and you might break the email. Changes **ripple** because everything touches everything. Cohesion is low, the methods don't actually belong together, so the class is hard to read, test, and reuse.

## The refactor: extract each concern

Give every responsibility its **own** class, and let a thin **coordinator** wire them together:

```
inv  = new Invoice(items);     // just the math
repo.save(inv);                // persistence
printer.toPdf(inv);            // layout
mailer.email(inv);             // delivery
```

Now `Invoice` holds only the business math, `InvoiceRepo` owns the database, `InvoicePrinter` owns layout, `InvoiceMailer` owns delivery. Each class has **exactly one reason to change**, cohesion is high, and a change to email rules can't possibly break tax math. Changes stay **isolated**.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-solid","activeLesson":"single-responsibility"}],["$","$L18","single-responsibility",{"lesson":{"id":"single-responsibility","patternId":"lld-solid","title":"Single Responsibility (S)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"A class should have ONE reason to change. Bundle one responsibility per class; split it the moment a class starts juggling many.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Atlassian"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The god class","code":["class Invoice {","    calcTotal(){ ... }   // business math","    saveToDB(){ ... }    // persistence","    toPdf(){ ... }       // layout","    emailTo(c){ ... }    // email","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Here is Invoice. It compiles, it works, and at a glance it looks like a perfectly normal class, just a busy one. So what is wrong with it?","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"inv","name":"Invoice","fields":["- items","- total"],"methods":["+ calcTotal()","+ saveToDB()","+ toPdf()","+ emailTo(c)"],"x":1,"y":0.4,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv"],"activeIds":["inv"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Look at its four methods. calcTotal() is business math, saveToDB() is the database, toPdf() is PDF layout, emailTo() is delivery. Four methods, and they touch four completely unrelated concerns.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv"],"activeIds":["inv"]},"vars":[["Invoice","does 4 jobs"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"That is FOUR reasons to change: tax rules, DB schema, PDF layout, email template, each owned by a different team. Touch one and you risk the others, because they all live in one body. That is the Single Responsibility violation.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv"],"activeIds":["inv"]},"vars":[["reasons to change","4 💥"]]}]},{"label":"One concern each","code":["class Invoice        { calcTotal(){...} }   // math only","class InvoiceRepo    { save(inv){...} }     // persistence","class InvoicePrinter { toPdf(inv){...} }    // layout","class InvoiceMailer  { email(inv){...} }    // delivery","// a coordinator wires them together"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Refactor. Invoice keeps only what is genuinely its own, the business math, calcTotal(). The database, the PDF, the email all move out. One concern: the numbers.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"inv","name":"Invoice","methods":["+ calcTotal()"],"x":1,"y":0.2},{"id":"repo","name":"InvoiceRepo","methods":["+ save(inv)"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"pdf","name":"InvoicePrinter","methods":["+ toPdf(inv)"],"x":1,"y":1.6},{"id":"mail","name":"InvoiceMailer","methods":["+ email(inv)"],"x":2,"y":1.6}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv"],"activeIds":["inv"]},"vars":[["Invoice","math only"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Persistence becomes InvoiceRepo with save(inv). The only reason it ever changes is the database, schema, queries, storage. Nothing else.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv","repo"],"activeIds":["repo"]},"vars":[["InvoiceRepo","persistence"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Layout becomes InvoicePrinter with toPdf(inv). Redesign the PDF all you want, the tax math and the database never even notice.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv","repo","pdf"],"activeIds":["pdf"]},"vars":[["InvoicePrinter","layout"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Delivery becomes InvoiceMailer with email(inv). A thin coordinator wires the four together: build the Invoice, repo.save it, printer.toPdf it, mailer.email it. Each class has exactly one reason to change, so changes stay isolated.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["inv","repo","pdf","mail"],"activeIds":["mail"]},"vars":[["InvoiceMailer","delivery"],["reasons each","1 ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
